          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In many
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+approaches+to+probability+theory"} categorical approaches to probability theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+spaces"} measurable spaces
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            part assigns to each space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valuation+%28measure+theory%29"} valuations
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            For probability theory, this can be interpreted as adding to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
            of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            new “random points”, modelled as probability measures or valuations. The old points, which we can think of as deterministic, are embedded in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            via the unit of the monad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to P X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+morphisms"} Kleisli morphisms
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            can be seen as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+maps"} stochastic maps
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+system"} extension system
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} monad in computer science
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+elements"} random elements
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/law+of+a+random+variable"} laws
            of the random elements.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} Algebras
            of probability and measure monads can be interpreted as generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+spaces"} convex spaces
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+spaces"} conical spaces
            of a certain kind. For
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variable"} random variable
          %p The details vary depending on the monad and on the category under consideration.
          %p
            Many choices of categories and of monads are possible, depending on which aspects of measure theory or probability one wants to study. See
            %a{:href => "#detailed_list"} the table below
            for more details.
          %p
            Note that “probability monad” and “measure monad” are not used here as technical terms with a definition. Here we just explain what most monads of this kind look like. (There are ongoing works which may give a general, precise definition of probability monad.) The term “probability monad” was coined by Giry (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#giry80"} here
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
          %h2#functor_unit_and_multiplication Functor, unit and multiplication
          %h3#the_functor_random_outcomes The functor: random outcomes
          %p
            The basic idea, that holds for all monads of this sort, is that of forming spaces of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+elements"} random elements
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/law+of+a+random+variable"} laws
          %p
            Consider a category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+spaces"} measurable spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %em probability monad
            assigns to each space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            which we can think of as a space of “laws of random outcomes on
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+monad"} distribution monad
              assigns to each
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+monad#finite_distributions"} space of finite distributions
              on it.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
              assigns to each
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad#definition"} space of probability measures
              over it.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+probabilistic+powerdomain"} extended probabilistic powerdomain
              assigns to each
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+probabilistic+powerdomain#spaces_of_valuations"} space of continuous valuations
              on it.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kantorovich+monad"} Kantorovich monad
              assigns to each
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kantorovich+monad#wasserstein_spaces"} Wasserstein space
              over it.
          %p
            (See also the table
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#detailed_list"} below
          %p
            A possible interpretation of probability monads, which uses the interpretation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            as encoding generalized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theories"} theories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            is a space of “formal generalized convex combinations” (in the sense of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convex space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+monad"} distribution monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi heads
                  %mo ,
                  %mi tails
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{heads,tails\}
            of possible outcomes of a coin flip, the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            contains for example the distribution that assigns probability
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/2
            to both outcomes. This can be thought of as the formal convex combination
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi heads
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi tails
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{1}{2}\, heads + \frac{1}{2}\,tails.
          %p
            On the morphisms, the functor gives for example the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushforward+of+measures"} pushforward of measures
          %h3#the_unit_deterministic_random_variables The unit: deterministic random variables
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            gives, for each space of outcomes, a map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to P X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+measure"} Dirac measure
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valuation+%28measure+theory%29#dirac_valuation"} Dirac valuation
          %p
            These can be seen as the laws of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deterministic+random+variable"} deterministic random variable
          %h3#the_multiplication_mixing_probability_measures The multiplication: mixing probability measures.
          %p
            The multiplication of the monad is a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P P X\to P X
            for all objects
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+mixture"} mixing
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/average"} averaging
            probability measures.
