          %p
            In other words, the “average” or “mixture” can be thought of as an assignment
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E:P P X\to P X
          %h2#algebras_expectation_values Algebras: expectation values
          %p
            According to the interpretation of probability monads in terms of “formal generalized convex combinations”, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
            of a probability monad are then spaces equipped with a way of evaluating these expressions to a result, which we can see as a generalized “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixture"}> mixture
            ”, “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/average"}> average
            ”, or “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"}> expectation value
            ”. (Expectation values play a very important role in probability: probability monads can encode them via their algebras.)
          %p
            In other words, algebras of probability monads can be thought of as generalizations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+sets"} convex sets
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+monad"} distribution monad
              are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+spaces"} convex spaces
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon+monad"} Radon monad
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
              convex subsets of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+topological+vector+spaces"} locally convex topological vector spaces
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kantorovich+monad"} Kantorovich monad
              are closed convex subsets of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
          %p
            (See also the table
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#detailed_list"} below
          %p
            If the measure are not required to be normalized, instead of (generalized) convex combinations one should think of linear combinations with non-negative coefficients, and so as algebras one gets a generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+spaces"} conical spaces
            instead.
          %h2#kleisli_morphisms_random_maps Kleisli morphisms: random maps
          %p
            The basic interpretation of a Kleisli morphism, i.e. a map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to P Y
            = succeed "." do
              %em map with a random outcome
          %ul
            %li
              For the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+monad"} distribution monad
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+maps"} stochastic maps
              (or
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+matrices"} stochastic matrices
            %li
              For the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernels"} Markov kernels
            %li …and so on.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli composition
            recovers exactly the
            %span.newWikiWord
              Chapman-Kolmogorov formula
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Chapman-Kolmogorov+formula"} ?
            for the composition of stochastic maps. In the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conditional+probability"} conditional probability
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi y
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p(z|x) = \sum_y p(z|y) \, p (y|x)
          %p for the discrete case, and as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Y
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p(A|x) = \int_Y p(A|y) \, d p(y|x)
          %p for the continuous case.
          %p
            (See
            %a{:href => "#giry80"} Giry’s original article
            for the details, as well as the introductions given in
            %a{:href => "#thesisperrone"} Perrone ‘18, Section 1.1
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#notesperrone"} Perrone ‘19, Example 5.1.13
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli categories
            of probability monads are often instances of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+categories"} Markov categories
          %h2#monoidal_structure_products_and_marginals Monoidal structure: products and marginals
          %p
            Probability monads are usually defined on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+categories"} cartesian monoidal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(X\times Y)
            has the interpretation of containing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joint+distributions"} joint distributions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joint+and+marginal+probability"} marginals
            by pushing forward along the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            projections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y\to X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y\to Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+dependence"} stochastic dependence
            may be discarded.
          %p
            Given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/marginal+distributions"} marginal distributions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+distribution"} product distribution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+monad"} monoidal structure
            on the probability monad (or equivalently, a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative strength
